  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Solve the absolute value inequality $|4z+2|>6.$",
  "worked_solution": "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $4z+2$ whose distance from zero is greater than $6.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4z+2 & <-6 & \\text{or} &  & 4z+2 & >6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve each inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4z+2 & <-6 &  &  & 4z+2 & >6 \\\\ 4z & <-8 &  &  & 4z & >4 \\\\ z & <-2 &  &  & z & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & <-2 & \\text{or} &  & z & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
